<TopicPage slug="subjects/history/advanced/historiography-debates-and-schools">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Historiography is <strong>the history of history-writing</strong> — the study of how each
    generation's assumptions, politics and available sources shape what it sees in the past.
    It is the discipline's self-awareness layer, and its first lesson is uncomfortable:
    history is not a record that is consulted, it is an <em>argument</em> that is conducted.
    The same archives sustain Gibbon's decaying Rome and Peter Brown's flourishing Late
    Antiquity; the same documents on 1914 have convicted Germany, acquitted it, and spread the
    blame across a sleepwalking continent. Reading the schools teaches you what every history —
    a textbook, a documentary, this atlas — is doing underneath its narrative: choosing a scale,
    a cast of characters and a causal engine. Herodotus already knew this; he called his
    enquiries <em>historia</em> because they were investigations, not transcripts.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="four schools, one discipline — each still active" widget="equationStep" config={histioCfg} />

  <h2>Ranke and the empirical revolution</h2>
  <p>
    Modern historiography begins with Leopold von Ranke, whose <em>Histories of the Latin and
    Germanic Nations</em> (1824) promised to show the past <em>wie es eigentlich gewesen</em> —
    as it actually was. The method mattered more than the slogan: work from original documents
    over secondary accounts, interrogate each source for its provenance and bias, and let the
    archive, not rhetoric, decide the argument. Ranke's seminar model — students trained on
    source criticism like laboratory scientists — spread across the world and remains the
    discipline's baseline discipline. But the promise concealed an assumption: that sources are
    windows rather than arguments. Every charter, letter and chronicle was itself written by
    someone, for someone, with an agenda; the "facts" arrive pre-selected. This is why the
    field's oldest mantra is that a primary source must be read <em>against</em> the grain as
    well as with it — the medieval chronicle that omits a famine tells you what the monastery
    wanted remembered, and the omission is evidence too. Charles Beard went further in 1934,
    arguing that even the most scrupulous historian writes from a time and place; the dream of
    the view from nowhere has been contested ever since.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "primary sources speak for themselves." They never do. A
    source is an artefact produced by an author with an audience and an aim, and the
    professional skill — source criticism — exists precisely because sources mislead:
    forgeries, flattery, selective memory, genre conventions. Two honest historians can hold
    the same document and report different facts from it, because "what it says" already
    involves interpretation.
  </p>

  <h2>Marx and the materialist school</h2>
  <p>
    If Ranke asked what happened, the Marxist school asked what <em>drove</em> it: for Marx,
    the engine of history is the mode of production — feudal, capitalist, and their
    transitions — and the class struggle those structures generate. The school's gift to
    historiography survives Marx's failed predictions: a insistence that politics and wars are
    surface weather above economic structures, that "the history of all hitherto existing
    society is the history of class struggles", and that historians should ask who owned what
    and who worked for whom. Its influence runs through everything from Christopher Hill's
    revision of the English Civil War (a bourgeois revolution, in his reading of the 1940s–50s)
    to Eric Hobsbawm's age-of-revolutions tetralogy. The school also produced one of
    historiography's model controversies: the <em>transition debate</em> over how feudalism
    became capitalism — Dobb blamed internal class relations, Sweezy pointed to trade, and the
    argument, running from the 1950s, taught a generation that a single "when did X begin"
    question can expose an entire theory of history.
  </p>

  <h2>The Annales: history from the depths</h2>
  <p>
    In 1929 Marc Bloch and Lucien Febvre founded the journal <em>Annales d'histoire
    économique et sociale</em> and quietly re-proportioned the discipline. Their heir Fernand
    Braudel divided time into three durations: the almost motionless <em>longue durée</em> of
    geography and climate, the slow rhythm of social and economic structures, and the flashy
    <em>histoire événementielle</em> of kings and battles — the politics other historians had
    mistaken for history itself. His <em>The Mediterranean and the Mediterranean World</em>
    (1949, drafted partly in a POW camp) spends a hundred pages on mountains and coastlines
    before any statesman appears, and the book's very architecture is its thesis: Philip II's
    policies mattered less than the sea lanes and harvest cycles they floated on. The cost of
    the school's founding conviction is part of its history: co-founder Marc Bloch — a Jewish
    historian, Resistance organiser and the author of <em>The Historian's Craft</em>, drafted
    in fragments while he fought — was executed by the Gestapo in June 1944, and the school's
    postwar dominance carries his memorial. The Annales
    descendants pushed further down the social scale: Le Roy Ladurie rebuilt a Pyrenean village
    from an inquisitor's register (<em>Montaillou</em>, 1975), and Carlo Ginzburg reconstructed
    the cosmology of Menocchio, a Friulian miller who was burned for his opinions
    (<em>The Cheese and the Worms</em>, 1976) — microhistory proving that even the illiterate
    majority, the people the archives recorded only when they came before a court, had worlds
    worth reconstructing.
  </p>

  <h2>History from below and the widening archive</h2>
  <p>
    The 1960s–80s widened the cast permanently. E. P. Thompson's <em>The Making of the English
    Working Class</em> (1963) restored "the enormous condescension of posterity" toward the
    artisans and labourers who made themselves into a class — agency, not just conditions.
    The Subaltern Studies collective (Ranjit Guha and colleagues, from 1982) asked what colonial
    archives could and could not say about the peasants of India, whose revolts appeared in
    British records only as disorder to be suppressed; Joan Scott's 1986 essay made gender a
    category of historical analysis, showing how every era's gender order structured its
    politics. The common move is the same: ask who the prevailing historiography left out, find
    the new sources (court records, petitions, oral testimony) that reach them, and treat their
    absence from the old story as itself a historical fact. The result is not that Rankean
    rigour was abandoned — it is that its object expanded from states to societies.
  </p>

  <h2>Debates as case studies: three arguments that never ended</h2>
  <p>
    The fastest way into historiography is to watch one question refuse to settle. <strong>The
    origins of the First World War</strong>: the Versailles settlement wrote German war guilt
    into Article 231; interwar revisionists spread the blame; in 1961 Fritz Fischer's
    <em>Griff nach der Weltmacht</em> used newly opened archives to convict Berlin of a
    deliberate bid for dominance, detonating a West German political scandal; Christopher
    Clark's <em>The Sleepwalkers</em> (2012) redistributed responsibility across all the great
    powers. Each generation found, in the same documents, the war its own century needed.
    <strong>The fall of Rome</strong>: Gibbon's eighteenth-century grand narrative of decay and
    barbarian catastrophe met Peter Brown's <em>Late Antiquity</em> — a slow transformation
    into a vividly Roman medieval world — with archaeology (climate proxies, plague pits)
    shifting the balance between the two. <strong>Quantification versus narrative</strong>:
    Robert Fogel's cliometrics (Nobel 1993) argued with counterfactual arithmetic that
    railroads mattered less to American growth than everyone assumed, and with Engerman's
    <em>Time on the Cross</em> (1974) that slave plantations were efficient — statistically
    impeccable, morally incandescent, and a permanent lesson that a number is also an argument.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "revisionism means falsifying history." Revision is the
    discipline's engine, not its disease: Fischer's case against Germany was revisionism, and
    it was correct scholarship even where it was overclaiming. The pejorative use of
    "revisionist" belongs to politics, not method. The legitimate worry is not revision but
    <em>relativism</em> — the postmodern suggestion (Hayden White's <em>Metahistory</em>, 1973,
    argued historical narratives share their shapes with literature) that emplotment makes all
    histories fictions. The working answer: interpretation is unavoidable, evidence is still
    sovereign, and the difference between a debate and a free-for-all is the archive.
  </p>

  <h2>The postmodern challenge</h2>
  <p>
    The sharpest theoretical attack came from within. E. H. Carr's <em>What Is History?</em>
    (1961) told the profession to "study the historian before you study his history" — facts
    become facts through selection; G. R. Elton answered that objective truth about the past
    was nonetheless achievable, and their exchange remains the field's cleanest statement of
    the objectivity problem. White's structural reading of narrative made historians uneasy
    about the stories they tell; Keith Jenkins pushed toward the conclusion that history is a
    textual practice of the present. White's specific claim was that historians
    <em>emplot</em> — that the same set of facts can be cast as romance, tragedy, comedy or
    satire, with the plot form chosen before the writing starts — so narrative shape is itself
    an interpretive act. Most working historians settled on a defensible
    middle: all history is constructed, but some constructions are <em>disciplined</em> —
    answerable to sources, falsifiable in detail, corrigible in public — and that discipline,
    not neutrality, is what separates history from myth. Postcolonial historiography added the
    political edge: whose past was constructed for whom, and in whose language — a question
    that reopened everything from museum collections to national curricula.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "objectivity means having no point of view." It means having
    a <em>disciplined</em> one. Every historian selects a scale and a question; the honest ones
    declare them and submit to the evidence's veto. The alternative — pretending to stand
    nowhere — produces not objectivity but smuggled assumptions, which is exactly what
    Herbert Butterfield caught in <em>The Whig Interpretation of History</em> (1931): the
    habit of writing the past as a slow march toward us, judging every earlier age by its
    resemblance to the present.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="check yourself: schools, sources and the habits of mind" widget="quiz" config={histioQuizCfg} />

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Memory versus history.</strong> Pierre Nora's <em>lieux de mémoire</em> project
    opened the study of how nations remember — commemoration, monuments, anniversaries — as a
    force that competes with scholarly history. The "history wars" over school curricula,
    museum displays and statues are historiography running in public: they are arguments about
    which past a society owes itself, and they will not be settled by more documents alone,
    because they are arguments about values wearing documents as armour.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The digital and global turns.</strong> Two recent transformations are redrawing the
    field's methods. Digitised archives and topic modelling allow historians to read corpora no
    lifetime could — the Republic of Letters' correspondence networks, millions of shipping
    records — turning quantitative history from a marginal school into a mainstream tool, with
    new failure modes (the archive is digitised unevenly, so the readable past is skewed toward
    the states that kept paperwork). And the global-history turn dismantles the nation-state as
    the default unit: histories of cotton, silver, cholera or the telegraph now run across
    empires, tracing the connections that older historiography, organised around Britain or
    France or Germany, could not see.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The standing questions.</strong> Where does explanation stop being history — can a
    historian "explain" 1914 or only narrate it? Is counterfactual reasoning legitimate
    (Fogel's railroads, Niall Ferguson's <em>Virtual History</em>) or a parlour game? Can
    history be scientific in any meaningful sense, and what would that even mean after Kuhn?
    Every generation restarts these arguments — which is the point: historiography is the
    proof that the discipline is alive, because a subject that stopped revising its own
    history would have stopped being a discipline at all.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Braudel, The Mediterranean; Jenkins, Rethinking History; Eley, A Crooked Line.</p>
